'''transpicuous''' (trȧn-spik′ū-us), ''a.'' [= It. ''traspicuo'', < L. as if *''transpicuus'', < ''transpicere'', see or look through, < ''trans'', through, + ''specere'', look: see ''spy''. Cf. ''conspicuous'', ''perspicuous''.] Transparent; pervious to the sight.

{{smaller block|That light,<br>Sent from her through the wide ''transpicuous'' air<br>To the terrestrial moon.{{right|''Milton'', P. L., viii. 141.}}}}

'''transpierce''' (trȧns-pērs′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''transpierced'', ppr. ''transpiercing''. [< F. ''transpercer''; as ''trans-'' + ''pierce''.] To pierce through; penetrate; pass through; transfix.

{{smaller block|He saw him wounded and ''trans-pierced'' with steele.{{right|''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 225.}}}}

{{smaller block|They . . . were often ''transpierced'', horse and rider, by the Moorish darts, impeding the progress of their comrades by their dying struggles.{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 91.}}}}

