{{smaller block|So far as the plant is concerned, three or four ''transplantings'' are better than one. {{right|''Science'', XIV. 364.}}}}

2. That which is transplanted.

{{smaller block|Such colonies become so intimately fused with others that not seldom the ''transplantings'' from them turn out impure. {{right|''Alien. and Neurol.'', X. 470.}}}}

'''transplendency''' (trȧn-splen′den-si), ''n.'' [< ''transplenden(t)'' + ''-cy.''] Supereminent splendor.

{{smaller block|The supernatural and unimitable ''transplendency'' of the Divine presence.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Antidote against Idolatry, ii.}}}}

'''transplendent''' (trȧn-splen′dẹnt), ''a.'' [< ''trans-'' + ''splendent.''] Resplendent in the highest degree.

{{smaller block|The clear crystal, the bright ''transplendent'' glass,
Doth not bewray the colours hid, which underneath it has.
{{right|''Wyatt'', Complaint of the Absence of his Love.}}}}

'''transplendently''' (trȧn-splen′dẹnt-li), ''adv.'' In a transplendent manner; with extreme splendor.

{{smaller block|The divinity, with all its adorable attributes, is hypostatically, vitally, and ''transplendently'' residing in this humanity of Christ.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Antidote against Idolatry, ii.}}}}
